<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hangzhou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lin'an</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hangzhou, known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> period as Lin'an, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. It became the capital of the Southern Song after the loss of northern China to the Jin. It grew into one of the largest and wealthiest cities of its time and fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> during the end of the Song Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hangzhou.jpg" alt="Hangzhou" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EditQ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9D%AD%E5%B7%9E%E9%92%B1%E6%B1%9F%E6%96%B0%E5%9F%8E_4_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hangzhou lies in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> province, at the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, which runs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in the south-central part of the Yangtze River Delta. The city centre is built around the eastern and northern sides of West Lake, just north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river">Qiantang River</a>, the largest river in Zhejiang. Each year a tidal bore races up the Qiantang through Hangzhou, reaching up to 12 m in height. The climate is humid subtropical, with long, hot, humid summers and chilly, drier winters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Hemudu culture inhabited Yuyao, 100 km south-east of Hangzhou, as far back as seven thousand years ago, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> was first cultivated in southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The jade-carving Liangzhu culture, named for its type site just northwest of the city, lived in the surrounding area around five thousand years ago. Hangzhou was established as a county seat in 221 BCE and later served as the capital of the Wuyue Kingdom (923 to 997).</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> the poet Bai Juyi, as governor, had a stronger and taller dyke built to hold the water of West Lake, which the surrounding farmland depended on, and had willows planted along it. In 1089 another poet-governor, Su Shi, used 200,000 workers to build a 2.8 km causeway across the lake from mud dredged from its bottom.</p><p>Hangzhou became the capital of the Southern Song dynasty after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> conquered most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in the Jingkang Incident of 1127. The surviving imperial family had retreated south, and Emperor Gaozong moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangqiu">Shangqiu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> before settling at Hangzhou. The city was besieged and captured by the Mongol armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> in 1276. The historian Patricia Buckley Ebrey noted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> spared Hangzhou and the Zhao royal family of the Southern Song, while they had killed the Jurchen Wanyan royal family by the hundreds at the Siege of Kaifeng in 1232. The new Yuan capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>), but Hangzhou remained an important commercial and administrative centre for the south.</p><p>The port remained important until the middle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, when its harbour slowly silted up. Under the Qing it held an imperial army garrison. The Taiping Heavenly Kingdom occupied the city in 1856 and 1860, and it was heavily damaged in its conquest, occupation and reconquest by the Qing army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hangzhou is the capital and most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> province and a sub-provincial city. Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> lie in or near it: the West Lake Cultural Landscape, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> and the Archaeological Ruins of Liangzhu City. Its native residents speak the Hangzhou dialect, a Wu dialect, and its cuisine is regarded as representative of Zhejiang cooking.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hangzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Song court re-formed in the south as the Southern Song (1127–1279), with its capital eventually fixed at <b>Lin'an</b> (modern Hangzhou).</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The remaining Song forces regrouped under the self-proclaimed Emperor Gaozong (1127–1162), who withdrew south of the Yangtze and established a new capital at Lin'an (modern <b>Hangzhou</b>).</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The canal linked Luoyang with Chang'an, with the agricultural centres towards Jiangdu (now Yangzhou) and Yuhang (now <b>Hangzhou</b>), and with the northern frontiers near modern Beijing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…fragments; their printers were the first after Bi Sheng to use movable type, in clay and in wood, and their Buddhist canon, mostly completed under Renzong, was printed in more than 3,620 juan at <b>Hangzhou</b> in 1302 under the Mongols for the monasteries of the old Tangut lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rice farmers built a walled city near present-day <b>Hangzhou</b> with a palace complex and flood defences, and its sharply ranked burials mark it as an early state.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most southerners were farmers, many of them tenants of great landlords, and townspeople of cities such as <b>Hangzhou</b>, the former Song capital and the largest city of the Yuan realm.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
